 81	## Standalone unit probe (U52)
 82	- Purpose: measure whether a CPU-only baseline's units gain throughput when run at once, right after setup, without the full reproduce and profile stages (about 10 h, near the 12 h ceiling, against about 3 h). Owner decision 2026-11-02: stage 2 runs standalone, after run10 (U51) showed that four median-cost units selected on their own reproduce run9's recorded result files byte for byte. The comparison is against that recorded baseline, with its existing limits; it says nothing about the shortest or longest units or about the scientific outcome.
 83	- Optional `args.units_probe` is an object with exactly the key `reference`, defined as for `args.units_check`. An invalid value, or both `args.units_probe` and `args.units_check` present, fails reproduce with `error_class: parse` before any box command. Without `args.units_probe`, reproduce and every later stage are unchanged.
 84	- In unit-probe mode reproduce runs the box probe, harness copy or generation, upload and setup with its repairs exactly as usual, and requires the unit files exactly as units-check mode does (`parse` before the harness upload). Before the harness upload it also takes the sample by the unit-mode sample rule and computes the SHA-256 of every output path of the four sample units under `reference`; a missing or unreadable reference file fails reproduce with `error_class: parse`. It writes those digests to `A/results/reproduce/units_reference.json` in the reference-file shape (see Concurrency probe, "Output checks").
 85	- After setup passes, the probe runs in place of the usual anchors step: through boxrun, as operation `concurrency` under the resolved `anchors` budget, from the shared run directory, one command that writes trusted `m5/concurrency_probe.py` and the reference file to remote `profiler/concurrency_probe.py` and `profiler/units_reference.json` (inline, as the profile stage writes the probe), removes any earlier `out/concurrency/` and `concurrency-work/`, and runs `python3 -I profiler/concurrency_probe.py --units harness/run_units.sh harness/units.json out/concurrency <budget_s> profiler/units_reference.json`. The probe is authored work: no repair and no identical retry. Fetched files land in `A/results/reproduce/out/concurrency/`; the log is `A/results/reproduce/<receipt-id>.log`.
 86	- Status: a timeout of the probe command, or a remaining budget of zero, fails reproduce with `timeout`; a failed fetch with `infra`; a nonzero exit, or a missing `concurrency.json` or one not valid as defined for unit mode, with `parse`. Otherwise reproduce is PASS whatever the outcome. Reproduce facts add optional `units_outcome` (the file's `outcome`, one of its six values), `units_factor_2` and `units_factor_4` (the n=2 and n=4 batch factors, each a finite positive number or null) and `units_shared_writes` (true when any arm that ran has `shared_changes` above 0, false when at least one arm ran and none has, null when no arm ran); all four are null outside unit-probe mode, on failure before a valid file, and on SKIPPED/stub records, and records without them stay valid. Unit-probe mode never runs the anchors step or `reproduce-freeze`, and every stage after reproduce except takeaways is SKIPPED, as after a failed stage.
120	- Unit mode (U50b). The probe script's second form, `python3 -I profiler/concurrency_probe.py --units <run_units_file> <units_json> <out_dir> <budget_s> <reference_json>`, runs unit mode. Only the standalone unit probe (U52) invokes it; the profile stage always runs command mode exactly as described above (owner decision 2026-11-02: the standalone route; unit mode inside the profile stage is deferred). Unit mode never uses MPS and does not look for the MPS binary. It compares the same four units run in one invocation, as separate invocations one after another, and as separate invocations at once, holding inputs and per-unit configuration fixed and checking every output byte for byte. Owner decision 2026-11-01: the smallest bounded experiment that resolves whether a CPU-only baseline's units gain throughput when run at once, with only the pipeline changes that comparison needs. Reader evidence (blind-01 run9): units are distinct inputs, selectable by restricting the prepared input list to original records (the command-line selector has no effect); each unit writes its own result files at the same paths in full and subset runs, with no run-specific content, and its outputs repeat bit for bit; the reproduce logs record every unit's cost; whether a unit reads state left by earlier units of the same invocation is unknown, which the comparison with the recorded baseline tests; four median-cost units selected on their own reproduced run9's recorded result files byte for byte (run10, U51); units take 1 to 10 min with a cost spread over 50%; the baseline's own threads already use at least half the logical CPUs. Command mode would instead run three whole commands of 1 to 2 h and compare no outputs.
121	- Sample: units sorted by `cost_s` ascending, ties by manifest order; the sample is the four consecutive sorted units starting at sorted position floor((N - 4) / 2), N being the number of units, kept in that order as sample positions 0 to 3. Units near the median cost keep concurrent invocations of similar length, so a batch runs at its full width for nearly its whole wall time; the factor therefore describes typical units, not the shortest or longest.
122	- Arms run in this fixed order, every invocation in a fresh work directory built as in command mode (named `<label>` for an arm with one invocation, `<label>-<j>` for invocation j = 1, 2, ... of an arm with several):
123	  - `a-1`: one invocation `bash harness/run_units.sh <id0> <id1> <id2> <id3>` (sample order), the baseline's own serial mode;
124	  - `b-1`: four invocations of one unit each, one after another in sample order;
125	  - `c2-1`: sample units 0 and 1 started together, then, after both exit, units 2 and 3 started together;
126	  - `c4-1`: all four started together;
127	  - `c4-2`, `c2-2`, `b-2`, `a-2`: the same arms again in reverse order, to balance drift.
128	  Each invocation's stdout and stderr go to `out/concurrency/logs/<work directory name>.log` of the shared run directory, and its environment is inherited unchanged. An arm's wall runs on a monotonic clock from launching its first invocation to the exit of its last.
129	- Output checks. The reference file `<reference_json>` is exactly `{"sha256": {<output path>: <64 lowercase hex digits>}}`, keyed by every output path of the four sample units and nothing else. After `a-1`, every output path of every sample unit under `a-1`'s `out/` must exist and have the SHA-256 recorded for that path (`baseline_match`); a file missing under `a-1`, a path missing from the reference, or a reference file that is unreadable or not of that shape is a mismatch. When `a-1` exits nonzero or `baseline_match` is false, no later arm runs. After every later invocation, its units' output files must be byte-identical to `a-1`'s (`outputs_match`, true when every later executed invocation matched). An arm counts when all of its invocations exit 0 and all of their compared files match.
130	- Budget: before each arm, that arm and every later arm are skipped when its estimate exceeds `0.9 * budget_s` minus the probe's elapsed seconds. The estimate is the sum of the sample's `cost_s` for `a-1`, and `a-1`'s measured wall for every later arm.
131	- Resource use. For each invocation, from `os.wait4` on its process (which includes its waited-for descendants): `cpu_s` (user plus system seconds), `max_rss_kb`, `nivcsw` (involuntary context switches), `inblock` and `oublock`. For each arm, sampled about once a second while it runs: `busy_fraction` (mean non-idle share of total CPU time from `/proc/stat`, idle including iowait), `mem_available_min_kb` (the minimum `MemAvailable` from `/proc/meminfo`) and `threads_max` (the largest sum of `Threads` from `/proc/<pid>/status` over the processes of the arm's invocations; each invocation starts its own session and process group). An arm's `cpu_s` is the sum over its invocations and `effective_cores` is that sum divided by the arm's wall. Memory bandwidth and caches are not measured.
132	- Shared writes. Each arm records `shared_changes`: the number of non-directory entries under the shared run directory, outside `out/`, `concurrency-work/` and `profiler/`, that appeared, disappeared, or changed size or modification time (nanoseconds) between a snapshot taken just before the arm's first launch and one taken just after its last exit. Snapshots use `lstat` and never follow symbolic links, and are taken outside the arm's wall clock. Readers of run10 (`run10_post`, `run10_post2`) found that the measured command writes into the shared run directory, which concurrent invocations reach through their links, but could not establish which paths, how they are named or whether units read them back; the byte checks catch only collisions that change compared outputs or fail an invocation. The count is evidence for interpreting a factor, not a check: it never changes an outcome. Writes outside the shared run directory are not observed.
133	- In unit mode `out/concurrency/concurrency.json` is written atomically with exactly `{mode, mps, mps_failed, outcome, units_total, sample, baseline_match, outputs_match, serial_wall_s, separate_wall_s, batches, arms, nproc, mem_total_kb, factor}`:
134	  - `mode`: `"units"`; `mps` and `mps_failed`: false;
135	  - `outcome`: the first that applies of `"invalid_units"` (manifest unreadable, invalid or with fewer than four units), `"failed"` (an executed invocation exited nonzero), `"baseline_mismatch"`, `"outputs_mismatch"`, `"skipped"` (no `c2` or `c4` arm counted), otherwise `"measured"`;
136	  - `units_total`: N, or null for `invalid_units`; `sample`: four rows `{index, cost_s}` in sample order, `index` being the manifest position, or `[]` for `invalid_units`. No unit identifier or output path appears in the file;
137	  - `baseline_match` and `outputs_match`: booleans, or null when not evaluated;
138	  - `serial_wall_s`: the mean wall of the counted `a` arms; `separate_wall_s`: the mean wall of the counted `b` arms; each a finite positive number or null;
139	  - `batches`: exactly two rows, n=2 then n=4, each exactly `{n, wall_s, all_ok, skipped, factor}`: `wall_s` is the mean wall of the executed `c<n>` arms or null when none ran, `skipped` is true when none ran, `all_ok` is true when at least one ran and every executed one counted, and `factor` is `serial_wall_s / wall_s` when `all_ok` is true and `outcome` is `measured`, otherwise null;
140	  - `arms`: exactly eight rows in the fixed order, each exactly `{label, wall_s, all_ok, skipped, cpu_s, effective_cores, busy_fraction, mem_available_min_kb, threads_max, shared_changes, invocations}`, with `label` from `a-1` to `a-2`; numbers are finite and nonnegative (`shared_changes` an integer), or null when the arm did not run; `invocations` lists the executed invocations in launch order, each exactly `{units, rc, wall_s, cpu_s, max_rss_kb, nivcsw, inblock, oublock}`, `units` being sample positions;
141	  - `nproc`: `os.cpu_count()`; `mem_total_kb`: `MemTotal` from `/proc/meminfo`;
142	  - `factor`: the largest non-null batch factor, or null.
143	  The factor compares the baseline's own serial mode with concurrent invocations over the same units, so per-invocation start-up costs count against it; `separate_wall_s` minus `serial_wall_s` shows that cost. The probe exits 0 whenever it writes the file and 2 when it cannot.
144	- The standalone unit probe (U52) validates a unit-mode file against the exact shape above, including each batch factor and `factor` as the largest batch factor. A measured unit-mode factor below 1.05 is a valid negative finding.

